    "patchSets": [
        {
            "number": 1,
            "revision": "ee0b3932e43c0374a27ad116c0dfcf52320f6cf4",
            "parents": [
                "6c16fc980206e9819f8defeb10ff9b3ee8c4a319"
            ],
            "ref": "refs/changes/85/83285/1",
            "uploader": {
                "name": "Alexander",
                "email": "alexander.hansen@9elements.com",
                "username": "pointbazaar"
            },
            "createdOn": 1756830608,
            "author": {
                "name": "Alexander",
                "email": "alexander.hansen@9elements.com",
                "username": "pointbazaar"
            },
            "kind": "REWORK",
            "comments": [
                {
                    "file": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
                    "line": 0,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Matt Spinler",
                        "email": "spinler@us.ibm.com",
                        "username": "spinler"
                    },
                    "message": "I believe that @ed@tanous.net had asked for anything in a vendor directory to have a manufacturer check in the probe statement.\n\nI would like to get that cleared up first.\n\nFor IBM, I'm not even sure there is an obvious mnfg field in the IBM specific eeprom format, but I would hope that wouldn't prevent them from being in a subdir if all the other IPMI format ones are moved."
                },
                {
                    "file": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
                    "line": 0,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Alexander",
                        "email": "alexander.hansen@9elements.com",
                        "username": "pointbazaar"
                    },
                    "message": "Can we revisit these patch chains in light of https://discordapp.com/channels/775381525260664832/819741065531359263/1415378743479894058\n\nTo me it looks like having this manufacturer check is not a hard requirement if we know who made the board, and often the manufacturer is already part of the filename."
                },
                {
                    "file": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
                    "line": 0,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Ed Tanous",
                        "email": "ed@tanous.net",
                        "username": "edtanous"
                    },
                    "message": "Not quite, I had asked for anything new or modified to have a manufacturer probe statement, and if people are modifying and moving files around, I suspect they have the systems to know who the manufacturer field is in the fru and update it.\n\nAnything that seems like I chained the probe statement updates to the folder structure is coincidental/because the author could probably fix it.\n\n\n> IBM specific eeprom format\n\nYou kind of answered this yourself in the question, but just to make my position clear, the IBM VPD file format IS the match on the manufacturer, because nobody except IBM will be putting that file format on their systems.\n\nThe goal here is that we correctly detect the hardware, with prejudice toward avoiding false positives (matching on the wrong hardware)."
                },
                {
                    "file": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
                    "line": 0,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Alexander",
                        "email": "alexander.hansen@9elements.com",
                        "username": "pointbazaar"
                    },
                    "message": "> and if people are modifying and moving files around, I suspect they have the systems to know who the manufacturer field is in the fru and update it.\n\nFor the asrock configs i moved them based on the filename which contains `asrock` string.\n\nDo you want me to do some further research here on asrock website or other sites to confirm based on the system names?\n\n\n> Anything that seems like I chained the probe statement updates to the folder structure is coincidental/because the author could probably fix it.\n\nDo you think changes like this one can be merged?\n\n\nEven if we end up moving a config into the wrong folder by accident, or having sub-optimal probe statement, at least we have attempted to create some structure.\n\n\nAlternatively, we can keep the flat structure, but i can imagine it may not scale that well as people loose the overview about their config files.\n\nAnd for some tasks it is essential to know at least some of the configs which are used in a given system. For example to configure associations correctly, which go across individual config files."
                },
                {
                    "file": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
                    "line": 0,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Ed Tanous",
                        "email": "ed@tanous.net",
                        "username": "edtanous"
                    },
                    "message": "> Do you want me to do some further research here on asrock website or other sites to confirm based on the system names?\n\nIdeally, yes, but I don't think it need to hold up this review.\n\n> Even if we end up moving a config into the wrong folder by acciden....\n\nExactly. The penalty for failure here isn't that bad."
                }
            ],
